1
00:00:01,100 --> 00:00:04,680
Welcome to Education Session 3: advanced strategies.

2
00:00:05,430 --> 00:00:10,580
Hedging and averaging are techniques experienced traders use to manage positions.

3
00:00:11,330 --> 00:00:16,930
Used well, they can help. Used badly, they can increase risk. So let's look at both carefully.

4
00:00:19,630 --> 00:00:23,700
Hedging means opening a position that offsets the risk of another one.

5
00:00:24,450 --> 00:00:29,660
The goal is not to make extra profit. It's to reduce your exposure to a price move.

6
00:00:32,370 --> 00:00:37,820
Example: you're long 1 lot of EUR/USD from 1.1000.

7
00:00:38,570 --> 00:00:43,650
The price falls to 1.0950, and you're $500 down.

8
00:00:44,400 --> 00:00:48,050
A big news release is coming, but you don't want to close the trade.

9
00:00:48,800 --> 00:00:55,840
So you open a short position of 1 lot. Now, whichever way the price moves, the two positions offset each other.

10
00:00:56,590 --> 00:01:01,870
Your loss is locked at about $500, until you close one of the two positions.

11
00:01:04,570 --> 00:01:10,440
A hedge can also be partial: a smaller opposite position that only reduces your exposure.

12
00:01:11,190 --> 00:01:19,980
Or it can use a related market. An investor holding many shares might sell a stock index, to protect against a broad market fall.

13
00:01:22,670 --> 00:01:30,360
The benefits: you can cut exposure quickly, protect open profits, and keep a long-term position through a short-term risk.

14
00:01:31,110 --> 00:01:38,100
The limitations: while fully hedged, you can't profit either. You pay the spread on both positions, and swaps on both.

15
00:01:38,850 --> 00:01:44,550
The hardest part is the exit. Close one side at the wrong moment, and the loss can grow again.

16
00:01:45,300 --> 00:01:51,140
Also, some brokers and regions don't allow hedging in the same account, so check your account rules.

17
00:01:53,830 --> 00:01:54,880
Now, averaging.

18
00:01:55,630 --> 00:02:00,970
Averaging means building a position in several steps, so your average entry price changes.

19
00:02:01,720 --> 00:02:05,110
There are two directions: averaging down, and averaging up.

20
00:02:07,800 --> 00:02:12,570
Averaging down means buying more as the price falls, to lower your average price.

21
00:02:13,320 --> 00:02:25,470
Example: buy 1 lot at 1.1000, then another lot at 1.0950. Your average price is now 1.0975.

22
00:02:26,220 --> 00:02:35,520
The price only needs to climb back to 1.0975 to break even, not all the way to 1.1000.

23
00:02:36,270 --> 00:02:44,590
But you now hold 2 lots. Each pip is worth $20, not $10. If the price keeps falling, losses grow twice as fast.

24
00:02:47,300 --> 00:02:52,630
Averaging up means adding to a position that's already in profit, as the trend continues.

25
00:02:53,380 --> 00:03:06,020
Example: buy 1 lot at 1.1000, and add another at 1.1050 as the trade works. Your average is now 1.1025.

26
00:03:06,770 --> 00:03:15,990
You add size only while the market proves you right. But a sharp reversal can erase gains quickly, because the extra lot was bought at a higher price.

27
00:03:18,700 --> 00:03:22,090
Whichever direction you use, averaging needs firm rules.

28
00:03:22,840 --> 00:03:27,840
Decide in advance the maximum total size, and the stop loss for the whole position.

29
00:03:28,590 --> 00:03:31,010
Check your free margin before every addition.

30
00:03:31,760 --> 00:03:38,090
And never keep adding to a losing trade without a limit. That's how small losses turn into margin calls.

31
00:03:40,770 --> 00:03:42,630
Let's bring the three sessions together.

32
00:03:43,380 --> 00:03:49,470
You now know how to read your account: balance, equity, margin, free margin, leverage and swaps.

33
00:03:50,220 --> 00:03:54,470
You can plan trades with stop losses, take profits, and pending orders.

34
00:03:55,220 --> 00:03:59,630
And you understand how hedging and averaging work, and the risks that come with them.

35
00:04:00,380 --> 00:04:07,600
With these foundations, you can make more informed trading decisions. Trade with a plan, and always manage your risk.
